  "vulnerability_information": "Hi Team,\nHope all is good!\n\n## Summary:\nWhile Conducting my research I discovered that I'm able to see anyone's `reactions received` on the notification bar of his account.\n\n\n==for the seek of your valuable time, I think if you watched the POC Video first before reading the report, things will be easier and then you can read the report for the details.==\n\n## Steps To Reproduce:\n* 1. Go to `https://try.discourse.org/` and create a new account\n* 2. Then go to your summary profile `https://try.discourse.org/u/{Your_Username}/summary` make sure to change `{Your_Username}` to your username, in my case my username is `testBugs` as you see in the below picture, and also observe on the below picture that you will have access to 5 tabs in your profile.\n\n{F2866672}\n\n* 3. Then go to any other user's summary profile, for example go to this user `https://try.discourse.org/u/codinghorror/summary` and observe in the below picture that you will have access to only 3 tabs if it's any other user's profile.\n\n{F2866675}\n\n\n\n\n## So basically from the above pictures you observed that you can't access the notifications bar of any other user on the browser cause there's no notification button to click on.\n\n## So the notification bar itself is indeed accessible to only the user of the account.\n\n# POC:\n\n\n* 1. Here's my Notifications\n\n{F2866693}\n\nMy notification looks good and accessible, nice, but what if we change the username on the URL `https://try.discourse.org/u/testbugs/notifications` to `https://try.discourse.org/u/codinghorror/notifications` to access his notification bar?\n\n* 2. Here's the attempt to access the notification bar of  the user whose username is `codinghorror`:\n\n{F2866701}\n\nnice To this point it looks good and secure and the server-side checks with the Tokens properly and restricts access to the notification bar if I'm not the owner of the account.\n\n\nbut while digging deep and deep into the notification bar I discovered that there are multiple endpoints on the notification bar:\n\n{F2866716}\n\n* 1)   /notifications/responses\n* 2)   /notifications/likes-received\n* 3)   /notifications/mentions\n* 4)   /notifications/edits\n* 5)   /notifications/reactions-received\n\n\n\n\nlet's do the same technique of changing the URL in an attempt to access other user notification functions:\n\n* 1. changing `https://try.discourse.org/u/testbugs/notifications/responses` to `https://try.discourse.org/u/codinghorror/notifications/responses`     gives `403 Forbidden`\n\n* 2. changing `https://try.discourse.org/u/testbugs/notifications/likes-received` to `https://try.discourse.org/u/codinghorror/notifications/likes-received`     gives `403 Forbidden`\n\n* 3. changing `https://try.discourse.org/u/testbugs/notifications/mentions` to `https://try.discourse.org/u/codinghorror/notifications/mentions`     gives `403 Forbidden`\n\n* 4. changing `https://try.discourse.org/u/testbugs/notifications/edits` to `https://try.discourse.org/u/codinghorror/notifications/edits`     gives `403 Forbidden`\n\n* 5. changing `https://try.discourse.org/u/testbugs/notifications/reactions-received` to `https://try.discourse.org/u/codinghorror/notifications/reactions-received`     gives ==200 OK==      :)\n\n\nso yeah the last endpoint which is `/notifications/reactions-received` is accessible to anyone to see,  just all he needs is to change the URL and he will be able to see any user's reactions-received on their notifications bar.\n\n\n\n# POC:\n\n{F2866817}\n\n## Impact\n\nAttackers can see any user's `reactions received` on the website, all they need to do is craft the URL as I demonstrated.\n\n\nBest,\n@smile_for_bugs",
